  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Art, photos, and mirrors are what turn an apartment into your home. But when you rent, every hole in the wall can feel like a potential deduction from your security deposit. The good news is you have more options than ever for hanging art without damage, and with a little planning you can decorate freely and still get your deposit back.</p>

<h2>Step One: Check Your Lease</h2>
<p>Before you buy anything, read the section of your lease about alterations, decorating, or wall hangings. Some leases allow a reasonable number of small nail holes. Others ban nails completely or require you to patch holes before moving out. If the language is vague, send your property manager a short email asking what is allowed. Having the answer in writing protects you later.</p>
<p>It also helps to understand how deposits work. In Ohio, Revised Code 5321.16 requires landlords to return the deposit, along with an itemized list of any deductions, within 30 days after the tenancy ends and you give back possession. Deductions are for damage beyond normal wear and tear. Whether small nail holes count is often a gray area, which is exactly why damage-free methods are worth it. For more, see our <a href="/blog/ohio-tenant-rights-guide/">Ohio tenant rights guide</a>. This is general information, not legal advice.</p>

<h2>Damage-Free Hanging Options</h2>
<h3>Adhesive picture strips</h3>
<p>Interlocking adhesive strips are the go-to option for many renters. They hold light and medium frames securely and are designed to remove without leaving marks when used correctly. A few tips make a big difference:</p>
<ul>
<li>Clean the wall with rubbing alcohol first, not household cleaner, so the adhesive bonds well.</li>
<li>Check the weight rating and use more strips than you think you need.</li>
<li>Press firmly for about 30 seconds, then wait the recommended time before hanging the frame.</li>
<li>Avoid textured walls, wallpaper, and freshly painted surfaces, which may not hold or may peel.</li>
<li>When removing, pull the tab slowly straight down along the wall, not outward.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Adhesive hooks</h3>
<p>For items with a wire or sawtooth hanger, adhesive hooks work well. Again, match the weight rating, and keep heavy or valuable items off adhesive entirely.</p>
<h3>Picture ledges</h3>
<p>A picture ledge is a narrow shelf that holds frames leaning against the wall. It takes only a couple of anchor points, but then you can display and swap out many pieces without adding a single new hole. It is a great choice if you like rearranging. Check your lease before mounting one, since it does require screws.</p>
<h3>Leaning art and mirrors</h3>
<p>Large art and floor mirrors can simply lean against the wall on the floor or on top of a dresser, console table, or bookcase. Layering a few frames of different sizes on a surface looks intentional and stylish. Just make sure heavy pieces are stable, especially if you have kids or pets.</p>

<!--products-->

<h3>Tension rods and freestanding displays</h3>
<p>Tension rods inside window frames or alcoves can hold lightweight hanging items like textile art or plants. Easels, tabletop stands, and freestanding gallery panels let you show art without touching the wall at all.</p>
<h3>Washi tape and clips for posters and prints</h3>
<p>For unframed prints and posters, low-tack washi tape or clip hangers are gentle options. Test a small piece in a hidden spot first, because some paints are more delicate than others.</p>

<h2>Things to Keep Off Adhesive</h2>
<p>Some items are too heavy or too valuable to trust to adhesive alone:</p>
<ul>
<li>Large mirrors or heavy framed glass</li>
<li>Shelves meant to hold weight</li>
<li>Anything hanging over a bed or couch where a fall could hurt someone</li>
<li>Irreplaceable art or family heirlooms</li>
</ul>
<p>For these, either lean them or ask your landlord about using proper hardware. Many landlords will say yes, as long as you patch holes when you leave.</p>

<h2>If You Do Use Nails</h2>
<p>If your lease allows it, small finishing nails or picture hooks with thin nails leave tiny holes that are easy to fill. A few tips:</p>
<ol>
<li>Use the smallest nail that will safely hold the item.</li>
<li>Plan your layout on the floor first, or tape paper templates to the wall, so you only put in holes once.</li>
<li>Use a level to get it right the first time.</li>
<li>Keep a small tub of spackle and a putty knife for move-out.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Protecting Your Deposit at Move-Out</h2>
<h3>Remove carefully</h3>
<p>Take down adhesive products slowly and properly. If paint lifts anyway, it is better to know before your walk-through than after.</p>
<h3>Patch small holes</h3>
<p>If allowed or required by your lease, fill nail holes with spackle, let it dry, and lightly sand it flush. Ask your property manager whether they want you to touch up paint. Mismatched paint can look worse than a filled hole, so many landlords prefer that you leave painting to them.</p>
<h3>Document everything</h3>
<p>Take photos of every wall on move-in day and again on move-out day. Date-stamped photos are your best evidence if there is a dispute. Our <a href="/blog/renting-an-apartment-checklist/">renting an apartment checklist</a> covers the full move-in and move-out documentation process.</p>

<h2>Styling Tips for Renter-Friendly Walls</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Go big with one piece.</strong> A single large print leaned on a console makes more impact than many small frames.</li>
<li><strong>Use mirrors to brighten.</strong> Placing a mirror across from a window bounces light around, which helps on gray Ohio winter days.</li>
<li><strong>Group by color or frame style.</strong> A cohesive grouping looks curated even when everything is on a ledge or dresser.</li>
<li><strong>Do not forget textiles.</strong> Rugs, throws, and curtains add color and personality without touching the walls.</li>
</ul>
<p>Decorating on a budget? Our guide to <a href="/blog/furnish-apartment-on-a-budget/">furnishing an apartment on a budget</a> has more ideas for making a rental feel personal without spending much. With the right tools and a little patience, you can have walls you love and still walk away with your deposit.</p>
